<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.17.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01165</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.17.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>45000e9a-edfe-4d2c-82cb-c20a49052458</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<para>For questions 1 – 3, match each sequence with one of the definitions. Note that only the part of the definition showing the relationship between the current term and the previous term is given so as not to give away the solutions.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> 6, 12, 18, 24 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(c(n)=c(n−1)+6\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>  2, 14, 98, 686 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(a(n)=7 \cdot a(n−1)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> 160, 80, 40, 20 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(b(n)=\frac12 b(n−1)\)</para>
<para>For questions 4 – 5, select the first five terms of each sequence. Determine whether each sequence is arithmetic, geometric, or neither.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(a(1)=7\), \(a(n)=a(n−1)−3\) for \(n\geq2\)  </item>
</list>
    <list>
      <item>7, 4, 1, –2, –5, geometric </item>
      <item>7, –21, 63, –189, geometric</item>
      <item>7, –21, 63, –189, arithmetic</item>
      <item>7, 4, 1, –2, –5, arithmetic</item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 7, 4, 1, –2, –5, arithmetic</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>\( c(1)=3\), \(c(n)=10 \cdot c(n−1)\) for \(n\geq2\)  </item>
</list>
    <list>
      <item>3, 30, 300, 3000, 30000, arithmetic</item>
      <item> 3, 30, 300, 3000, 30000, geometric</item>
      <item> 3, 13, 23, 33, 43, geometric</item>
      <item> 3, 13, 23, 33, 43, arithmetic</item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis> Answer:</emphasis> b. 3, 30, 300, 3000, 30000, geometric</para>
<para>For questions 6 – 8, match each sequence with one of the recursive definitions. Note that only the part of the definition showing the relationship between the current term and the previous term is given so as not to give away the solutions.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> 3, 15, 75, 375, . . . </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(c(n)=5 \cdot c(n−1)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> 18, 6, 2, \(\frac23\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(a(n)=\frac13 a(n−1)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item> 17, 13, 9, 5 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(b(n)=b(n−1)−4\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item> An arithmetic sequence \(k\) starts 12, 6, . . . and is represented by the graph below. </item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b9796b5a8c6fd4e1dbe3f5a42c3f01ad31965ef5"/></media></figure></para>
<para><span>Which of the following is a recursive definition for sequence \(k\) when \(n\geq2\)?</span></para>
<list>
  <item>\(k(1)=12, k(n)=k(n)-6
    \)</item>
  <item><span>\(k(1)=12, k(n)=k(n-1)-6
      \)</span></item>
  <item>\(k(1)=12, k(n)=k(n-1)+6\)</item>
  <item>\(k(1)=12,6 \cdot k(n)=k(n-1)
    \)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(k(1)=12, k(n)=k(n-1)-6\)</para></section>
</content>
